    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (79.47) and **Daulton Varsho** (71.99) headline our lineup as the premier offensive anchors. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a massive 100% stolen base probability factor against a slow delivery from Valdez, while **Daulton Varsho** leverages a premium SP skill matchup against Houser, further bolstered by an 87% stolen base probability indicator. **Jonathan Aranda** (64.00) and **Dylan Beavers** (63.28) also provide strong value; **Jonathan Aranda** is positioned well to exploit a favorable dynamic platoon advantage against Perkins, and **Dylan Beavers** finds himself in a high-efficiency spot where both his platoon advantage and base-stealing potential drive his score well above his base projection.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Gavin Sheets**, who narrowly missed the cut with a 47.64 score. While he holds a solid platoon edge against May, he lacks the secondary category upside found in our lower-order starters, specifically the stolen base ceiling that is prioritized given our team's structural scarcity. Elsewhere, we are monitoring **Lawrence Butler**, whose score remains respectable at 55.35 despite a challenging matchup against Rasmussen, whose 3.17 xERA poses the stiffest SP resistance in our daily profile. \n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding **Cal Raleigh** and **Justin Crawford**, both of whom are pushed down the order and limited by unfavorable platoon dynamics or lower-tier lineup positioning. While **Cal Raleigh** remains a start due to roster necessity, the optimization process correctly benched **JJ Bleday** and **Jung Hoo Lee**, as their respective platoon and matchup concerns rendered them inferior to the high-floor, speed-weighted options deployed today. We remain vigilant on all \"Lineup Pending\" statuses, as any late-breaking shifts in the batting order could necessitate immediate adjustments to our efficiency projections.",
